------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Resolved: P37 non-restartability is intentional
--
-- P37 explicitly disables restart protection at entry with
-- TC PHASCHNG / OCT 4 and the comment "P37 IS NOT RESTARTABLE"
-- (Comanche055/P37_P70.agc line 113). The entire computation
-- (conic iteration up to 600+ loops, optional precision refinement,
-- Lambert targeting) runs unprotected. P70 (LM DPS abort) follows
-- the same pattern, clearing groups 1, 3, 4 and 6.
--
-- This is a deliberate design decision, likely to save code space.
-- A GOJAM during P37 loses all computation; the crew must manually
-- re-enter V37E37E and all input data. The vulnerability window
-- spans the entire multi-second computation.
--
-- Mission-level implication: during Apollo 13, a restart during P37
-- would have forced recomputation of the return trajectory. The
-- mission relied on ground-computed parameters to minimise onboard
-- P37 usage during the crisis.

open question "The CSI/A iteration uses seven distinct alarm codes (600-606) for different failure modes. The source comments do not clearly document which alarm corresponds to which failure. A mapping between CSIALRM values and the specific convergence failure they indicate would clarify the alarm handling logic."

open question "P34/P74's transfer angle exclusion zone (170-190 degrees) is only verified by crew procedure, not enforced by the software. If the crew proceeds with a near-180-degree transfer angle, the Lambert solver may produce inaccurate or divergent results. Should the spec model this as a soft constraint or document the expected degradation?"
